{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T2-003", "approach": "B1", "approach_base": "B1", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Faraday makes the water visible by condensing it as a drop and then testing it with potassium, which burns violently on contact with water; he shows carbonic acid by passing the candle's air through lime-water, which turns milky.\n\n- In Lecture III, he condenses water from the candle's products under a vessel of ice and salt, collecting a drop at the bottom of the dish.\n- To prove it is water, he uses potassium (discovered by Sir Humphrey Davy), which lights up and burns with a violent flame when thrown into water.\n- In Lecture V, he prepares clear lime-water from quick-lime and common water.\n- Ordinary air (oxygen and nitrogen) leaves the lime-water perfectly clear, even when shaken with it.\n- But when the lime-water contacts the general products of the candle, it quickly becomes milky, forming a white powder identical to chalk or whitening.\n- This shows the candle emits carbonic acid, the same substance that would issue from red-hot chalk with moisture.", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 1018 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o033", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b93"], "kind": "unit", "score": null, "rank": 0, "span": [59246, 60264], "meta": {}}, {"text": "[stripped: 2420 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o037", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b203"], "kind": "unit", "score": null, "rank": 1, "span": [137900, 140320], "meta": {}}], "evidence_units": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en