"aa11d6c898b9bdfa106d9c97b01b47110eceb2c9"}}, "structured": null}
{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T3-001", "approach": "B1", "approach_base": "B1", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Franklin's father, a tallow-chandler, made candles from tallow, which Faraday identifies as the fat of the ox (Russian suet) [1][2].\n\nTo turn it into stearin: the fat or tallow is first boiled with quick-lime to make a soap, then the soap is decomposed by sulphuric acid, which removes the lime and leaves the fat re-arranged as stearic acid, with glycerin produced at the same time; the oil is then pressed out and the remaining substance melted and cast into candles [2].", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 1970 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::o015", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::p1_b82"], "kind": "unit", "score": null, "rank": 0, "span": [30990, 32960], "meta": {}}, {"text": "[stripped: 1936 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b42"], "kind": "unit", "score": null, "rank": 1, "span": [5820, 7756], "meta": {}}], "evidence_units": ["K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::p1_b82", "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b42"], "evidence_items": [{"doc_id": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::o015", "kind": "unit", "chars": 1970, "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::p1_b82"], "cut": false}, {"doc_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::o029", "kind": "unit", "chars": 1936, "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-14474_faraday_chemical-history-of-a-candle.en::p1_b42"], "cut": false}], "evidence_chars": 4214, "llm_calls": 1, "embed_calls": 0, "prompt_tokens": 1177, "completion_tokens": 122, "embed_tokens": 0, "llm_wall_ms": 3269.3, "load_ms": 0, "latency_ms": 3282.8, "failed_calls": 0, "error": null, "trace": {"gold_units": 2, "missing_gold_units": [], "evidence_items": 2, "last_item_cut": false}, "models": {"chat": "openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash", "embed": "bge-m3:latest"}, "config_hash": "ebaf1eaeccc2", "index_fingerprint": "69cc31b8fde8cfec2d95d8dc0e634ed924e78ae2", "ts": "2026-09-26T02:02:39", "run_id": "20260926-020214-d3c4cb", "lock_wait_ms": 0, "split": "dev", "meta": {"answer_format": "v2_explanation", "answer_prompt": "answer-v2-explanation-v1", "answer_num_predict": 640, "ollama_version": null, "questions_file": "dev_v2run_BOOK-en.jsonl", "final": false, "docs_restriction": null, "pool": {"pool_id": "BOOK-en", "manifest_sha1": "aa11d6c898b9bdfa106d9c97b01b47110eceb2c9"}, "identity": {"experiment_id": "datasets-v2-dev", "question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T3-001", "pool_id": "BOOK-en", "method": "B1", "options": {"flags": {}, "variants_version": "variants-v2"}, "reader": {"chat": "openrouter/z-ai/glm-5.3-flash", "chat_digest": "openrouter"}, "replicate": 0, "setting": "en-en", "rid": "743ffecde853c94c4820", "code_sig": "094d16a548662280", "question_sig": "cd0cf1490c662628", "pool_sha1": "aa11d6c898b9bdfa106d9c97b01b47110eceb2c9"}}, "structured": null}
{"question_id": "V2-dev-BOOK-en-T3-002", "approach": "B1", "approach_base": "B1", "flags": {}, "setting": "en-en", "q_lang": "en", "d_lang": "en", "corpus": "K", "corpus_set": "BOOK_en", "answer_text": "Franklin - model: an odd volume of the Spectator (the third), whose writing he thought excellent and wished to imitate\nFranklin - practice: made short hints of the sentiment in each sentence, laid them by a few days, then tried to complete the papers again in his own words and compared his version with the original to find and correct faults; he also turned tales into verse and back into prose, and jumbled his hints into confusion to re-order them to teach himself method\nDouglass - model: the letters ship carpenters wrote on timber (L, S, L. F., S. F., L. A., S. A.), the Italics in Webster's Spelling Book, and Master Thomas's copy-books\nDouglass - practice: copied the letters with a lump of chalk on the board fence, brick wall, and pavement; challenged boys who could write to beat his letters to get lessons; copied the Italics until he could make them all without looking at the book; and wrote in the spaces left in Master Thomas's copy-book while his mistress was at class meeting\n\nComparison: Franklin used a published literary work (the Spectator) and practised by reconstructing and comparing prose, while Douglass used letters, a spelling book, and a child's copy-books, practising by copying with chalk on fences and walls and in leftover spaces — Franklin imitated and recreated whole papers, whereas Douglass learned letters and handwriting by copying.", "abstained": false, "retrieved_units": [{"text": "[stripped: 2361 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en", "section_id": "K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::o017", "unit_ids": ["K.gutenberg-20203_franklin_autobiography.en::p1_b133"], "kind": "unit", "score": null, "rank": 0, "span": [47041, 49402], "meta": {}}, {"text": "[stripped: 2275 chars]", "doc_id": "K.gutenberg-23_douglas